{#
  Container `resources:` requests and limits — the four-quantity block ~100 container specs
  across the k8s roles share, differing only in the values.

  NOT the same key as two other `resources:` in this tree, and this macro is for neither:
  a PersistentVolumeClaim's `spec.resources.requests.storage` (see claim-default.yaml.j2), and an RBAC
  Role's `rules[].resources`, which is a list of API resource names. Both sit at a different
  depth and neither has cpu/memory under it.

  Indentation is fixed at 10 spaces, the container-list depth in a Deployment, DaemonSet,
  StatefulSet or CronJob pod template here — 106 of the fleet's 108 container blocks. The two
  nested deeper (a CronJob's `jobTemplate`) stay inline, for the same reason the securityContext
  macro leaves its two: a macro that can emit at two depths can emit at the wrong one.

  image-builder's build Job is outside `_k8s_render.rendered_docs()`, being caller-rendered.
  test_namespace_resource_defaults.py's
  `test_the_roles_outside_the_render_walk_are_accounted_for` renders it with a caller's
  variables instead and reads the parsed `resources`, so this macro hides nothing from that
  check. It asserts requests and limits appear together, because a request with no limit takes
  the namespace default and is REFUSED at admission if the request is higher — surfacing hours
  later as a failed image build.

  QUANTITIES ARE EMITTED QUOTED. Every value in this family is a Kubernetes quantity string —
  `250m`, `128Mi`, `1` — so quoting is a no-op to the parsed YAML while making the emitted form
  uniform.

  Arguments:
    cpu_limit, mem_limit, cpu_request, mem_request
        the four quantities, as values not as pre-quoted text.
    extra   an extended-resource NAME (jellyfin's and tdarr's `*_k8s_dri_resource`) to request
            and limit at 1. Kubernetes REJECTS an extended resource whose request and limit
            differ, so it is deliberately one argument emitting both rather than two that could
            drift apart.
#}
{% macro container_resources(cpu_limit, mem_limit, cpu_request, mem_request, extra=none) %}
          resources:
            limits:
              cpu: "{{ cpu_limit }}"
              memory: "{{ mem_limit }}"
{% if extra %}
              {{ extra }}: 1
{% endif %}
            requests:
              cpu: "{{ cpu_request }}"
              memory: "{{ mem_request }}"
{% if extra %}
              {{ extra }}: 1
{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
